"Represents project activity dependencies so that feasible sequence, critical paths and schedule flexibility can be examined.\n\nUse when: Find which dependencies determine completion time. Review parallel work and the effect of changing an activity duration.\n\nAvoid when: The main need is displaying organizational responsibility or a business-case decision tree.\n\nVisual grammar: Activity nodes identify bounded work and durations. Directed links encode defined precedence relationships and any lags. Critical-path and float annotations follow a stated schedule calculation.\n\nBuild: List activities with consistent duration and calendar assumptions. Connect only real dependencies and identify start and finish conditions. Calculate earliest and latest times, then inspect critical and near-critical paths.\n\nCheck: The precedence network has no unintended directed cycle. Link types and lag meanings are explicit. Criticality is calculated for the current schedule assumptions.\n\nPitfalls: Calling the longest-looking drawn path the critical path. Adding arbitrary links merely to make the drawing visually linear.\n\nExample: A conference setup network shows staging and catering proceeding in parallel before inspection and doors opening, exposing the completion constraint.\n\nAlternatives: Gantt chart, Critical-chain schedule."
